# watchOS chip hit testing

## Problem (learned)

On watchOS, `.buttonStyle(.plain)` only hits **opaque** label pixels. A chip with `Color.clear` (or empty space around caption text) looks tappable but ignores most taps — especially inside `ScrollView`. Offline/Cloud Connect chips failed this way.

## Rules

- **Opaque fill always**: Selected = accent (or solid). Unselected = `.ultraThinMaterial` or another opaque fill. Never `Color.clear` as the chip background for tappable controls.
- **Full-frame hit target**: Add `.contentShape` matching the chip clip shape after the frame/background.
- **Prefer proven patterns**: Match `CareControls` / `CareMlAmountGrid` / `DiaperKindGrid` (material fill + frame height `BabyTokens.careChipHeight`).
- **ScrollView chips**: If plain `Button` still misses taps, use full-frame `.onTapGesture` + `.accessibilityAddTraits(.isButton)` instead of plain Button.
- **Contract helper**: Use `ConnectPresetChipHit.fill(isSelected:)` — unselected must be `.material`, never `.clear`. Keep `connectPresetChipsUseOpaqueFillForHitTesting` green.

## Bad vs good

```swift
// BAD — only the text glyphs receive taps
Button(action: action) {
    Text(title)
        .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)
        .frame(height: BabyTokens.careChipHeight)
        .background(on ? palette.accent : Color.clear)
}
.buttonStyle(.plain)

// GOOD — full chip is tappable
Text(title)
    .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)
    .frame(height: BabyTokens.careChipHeight)
    .background {
        if on { palette.accent }
        else { Rectangle().fill(.ultraThinMaterial) }
    }
    .contentShape(shape)
    .clipShape(shape)
    .onTapGesture(perform: action)
    .accessibilityAddTraits(.isButton)
```
